-- S-instantiated-reselect (#1909), the ACCEPT shape, INFERRED channel -- the
-- OD6(a) twin of s-instantiated-reselect-declared.mdk.
--
-- Byte-for-byte that fixture with the SIGNATURE LINE DELETED.  DICT-SEMANTICS
-- OD6(a) says a declared context and the identical inferred one must adjudicate
-- the same way, so the hand-derived answer is the same: `boolW/intW`.
--
-- 🚨 IT IS A SEPARATE FIXTURE BECAUSE IT IS A SEPARATE WRITER.  The declared
-- context is registered by `registerMember`; the inferred one by
-- `registerInferredFor`, which is a different function reached on a different
-- pass, and BOTH were ids-only.  Patching one leaves the other statically
-- committed, at exit 0, with the declared/inferred pair silently disagreeing --
-- exactly the shape #1869 and #1161 both landed in.  MEASURED at the pre-slice
-- base `bffced42` on this tree: `boolW/boolW`, identical to its declared twin.
--
-- ⚠️ DO NOT REPLACE THIS FIXTURE WITH THE OBVIOUS ONE.  The natural inferred
-- probe adds a general `impl Tag (Wrap a)` beside the ground impls (that is what
-- #1909's own `c_1909_inferred` repro does), and it is MIS-SCOPED: with a general
-- impl in scope the residual `Tag (Wrap t)` is DISCHARGED by residual reduction
-- at generalization time, the binding keeps no predicate and therefore gets no
-- dict slot under any #1909 fix at all.  Confirmed on the unmodified base binary:
-- `check --types` prints `nest : a -> String`, with no context to carry.  The
-- general impl has to be ABSENT for the inferred binding to retain a predicate,
-- which is why this file has only the two ground impls.
--
-- ⚠️ TWO `impl Tag` BLOCKS, so Section 4 derives a permutation pair.  At base the
-- two declaration orders printed `boolW/boolW` and `intW/intW` here too.
data Wrap a = Wrap a

interface Tag t where
  tagOf : t -> String

impl Tag (Wrap Bool) where
  tagOf _ = "boolW"

impl Tag (Wrap Int) where
  tagOf _ = "intW"

nest x = tagOf (Wrap x)

main = println "\{nest True}/\{nest 1}"
